Turn on the graphics console as soon as the kernel asks for text mode.

On a board with a serial port none of this matters: kprintf goes out of the
UART from PE_init_kprintf() onwards and the framebuffer console is a luxury
that can wait for the VM system. With no serial port the framebuffer console
is the only console, and xnu does not light it up until kPEAcquireScreen,
which vcattach() issues from PE_init_printf(TRUE) -- after vm_init, deep
inside kernel_bootstrap_thread.

Everything printed between PE_create_console() and that point therefore goes
only into the message buffer, to be replayed later. If the kernel dies before
the replay, the screen still holds whatever the booter drew and the log is
sitting in memory with no way out. That is the entire early bring-up window,
and it is the window that fails.

The fix is one call: in text mode, enable the console at once rather than
waiting to be acquired.

It is safe this early:

  - gc_initialize(), a few lines above in the same function, has already run
    VCPUTC_LOCK_INIT(), so the console lock exists.
  - the only part of gc_enable() that touches the allocator -- the scrollback
    buffers -- is already behind an "if (vm_initialized)", which is false
    here, so nothing is allocated.
  - vc_render_font() returns immediately while vm_initialized is false, so
    the font cache stays empty, and vc_paint_char_32() already handles that
    by rendering each glyph into a static buffer instead.
  - gc_acquired is left alone, so vcattach() still does its own acquire,
    reallocates the buffers properly and replays the message buffer.

Graphics mode is untouched: a device that boots to a boot logo still does.
This only changes the path taken when the booter asks for text, which on this
port is what boot_args.Video.v_display = 0 selects.

The visible effect is that the screen clears and fills with the kernel log
from PE_create_console() onwards, which is also a useful signal in itself:
if the booter's own output is still on the panel, the kernel did not get
that far.
--- a/osfmk/console/video_console.c	2026-10-21 19:56:53.626448445 +0300
+++ b/osfmk/console/video_console.c	2026-10-21 19:57:01.986247753 +0300
@@ -2936,6 +2936,27 @@
 
 		case kPETextMode:
 			gc_graphics_boot = FALSE;
+			/*
+			 * Bring-up on a board with no serial port: the graphics
+			 * console is the only console there is, and nothing is
+			 * drawn on it until kPEAcquireScreen, which vcattach()
+			 * issues only once the VM system is up. Everything the
+			 * kernel prints between PE_create_console() and there is
+			 * lost -- and on a new port that stretch is precisely the
+			 * one that fails, so the screen stays frozen on whatever
+			 * the booter left and says nothing about why.
+			 *
+			 * Enabling the console here is safe this early. The lock
+			 * was initialized by the gc_initialize() above, every part
+			 * of gc_enable() that needs the allocator is already
+			 * guarded by vm_initialized, and vc_paint_char_32() renders
+			 * into a static buffer while the font cache is empty.
+			 * vcattach() still performs its own acquire afterwards and
+			 * replays the message buffer, so nothing is lost twice.
+			 */
+			if (!gc_acquired) {
+				gc_enable(TRUE);
+			}
 			break;
 
 		case kPEAcquireScreen:
